e polls, and prediction markets giving Jermaine Johnson only a 5-6% chance all point to a Republican hold of this open seat. Johnson's best fundraising quarter and the October debates offer openings, but no polling or rating evidence shows the race competitive.", "watch": "A standout debate performance or a wave of Democratic outside spending could consolidate late undecided voters and tighten the race in the final weeks.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Henry McMaster (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "South Carolina's governorship is open this year as Henry McMaster steps down under term limits, and Attorney General Alan Wilson enters the general election as the clear favorite over State Representative Jermaine Johnson. The state leans Republican by about eight points, and every independent rating outfit places the race in the Solid or Safe Republican column. Three polls since July, including one from early October, all show Wilson ahead by eight to ten points.\n\nPrediction markets give Johnson roughly a five to six percent chance of winning. His campaign posted its strongest fundraising quarter yet in the fall, and the candidates met in a series of debates in early October, giving the Democrat chances to introduce himself to a wider audience. Still, no public evidence shows the race narrowing, and Wilson's name recognition and the state's partisan lean keep him in a strong position heading into election day.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0588, "margin": -9.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0546, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0722, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0597, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0544, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0532, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0678, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0437, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0669, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "202